    {
      "filename": "The true burial place of Lady Grange - geograph.org.uk - 1042767.jpg",
      "title": "File:The true burial place of Lady Grange - geograph.org.uk - 1042767.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/49/The_true_burial_place_of_Lady_Grange_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1042767.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_true_burial_place_of_Lady_Grange_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1042767.jpg",
      "width": 640,
      "height": 427,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "John Allan",
      "description": "The true burial place of Lady Grange. This is the stone that marks the burial place of Lady Grange. It stands in the cemetery at Trumpan Church in Waternish. Born as Rachel Cheislie in 1678, her remains were buried here in 1745 after a terrible series of events instigated by her husband - James Erskine, the Scottish Lord Advocate.\nLord and Lady Grange lived in Edinburgh until their marriage broke down in 1730, at which point Lady Grange moved out, mainly because of Lord Grange's affairs while he was away in London as an MP. After the separation, Rachel took to standing outside his house shouting obscenities and being a general embarrassment. She also threatened to reveal that her husband had been involved in the Jacobite rising of 1715.\nIn April 1732 her husband arranged, with help from other Jacobites, for Rachel to be abducted from her home in Edinburgh. She was first taken to Linlithgow and thence to Polmaise Castle NS7791, where she was held for four months. After a time of being moved around the Highlands, she was put on the island of Haskeir NF6181 where she was kept for two years. In May of 1734 she was moved even further away, this time to Hirta, St Kilda, NF1099. There she was held incommunicado for the eight years until 1742. During that time, Lord Grange announced her death and held a false funeral in Edinburgh.\n\nRachel managed to get a note out from St Kilda to her cousin, who sent a gunboat to rescue her. Before it arrived though, the poor woman had been moved again, this time via Assynt to Skye, where she was left alone to survive her final three miserable years in a local cave.I hope she is resting in peace.",
      "dateCreated": "2008-11-12",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "trumpan-church-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "gf5r",
      "smallPath": "_m/g/f/5/r/trumpan-church-wp/the-true-burial-place-of-lady--small.webp"
    },
    {
      "filename": "View from Trumpan Church - geograph.org.uk - 1042759.jpg",
